WEBVTT

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:05.165
Security practitioners do not need quantum physics expertise to understand the migration

00:00:05.165 --> 00:00:05.865
problem.

00:00:05.865 --> 00:00:09.460
The key idea is that some quantum algorithms change the difficulty of certain mathematical

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:10.673
problems used in cryptography.

00:00:10.673 --> 00:00:13.420
Shor's algorithm is important because, at a high level,

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:17.480
it threatens public-key schemes based on factoring and discrete logarithms.

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:20.910
That includes RSA, classic Diffie-Hellman, and elliptic curve systems.

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:25.557
If a sufficiently large and reliable cryptographically relevant quantum computer existed,

00:00:25.557 --> 00:00:29.160
Shor's algorithm would undermine assumptions behind many public-key systems.

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:31.640
That does not mean attackers can do this at scale today.

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:33.570
It means systems being built, purchased,

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:37.135
or archived today may still be in use when capability changes.

00:00:37.135 --> 00:00:40.550
Planning is about reducing that future exposure before the timeline becomes urgent.

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:43.440
Grover's algorithm affects symmetric cryptography differently.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.050
It can provide a quadratic speedup for brute-force search,

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:49.980
which changes the security margin but does not collapse symmetric encryption in the same

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:52.760
way Shor's algorithm affects many public-key schemes.

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:53.380
In practice,

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:57.470
this often leads to discussions about using sufficiently strong symmetric key sizes rather

00:00:57.470 --> 00:01:00.290
than redesigning every symmetric primitive from scratch.

00:01:00.290 --> 00:01:02.955
The safest practitioner posture is balanced.

00:01:02.955 --> 00:01:06.840
Avoid dismissing quantum threats because the hardest attack is not practical today.

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:11.220
Also avoid claiming that quantum computers already break every system or make all security

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:12.195
controls obsolete.

00:01:12.195 --> 00:01:16.740
The real work is risk-based migration: know where vulnerable public-key cryptography sits,

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:18.675
understand data and system lifetimes,

00:01:18.675 --> 00:01:21.120
and build the ability to change cryptography deliberately.
